Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Valve Thirty-One Forgets the Downbeat
- Story & Context: The debut runs at 160 BPM. Emi opens with a sustained six-tone shō lattice containing four ANCHOR tones and two MOBILE tones. Malik programs a footwork cell whose kick triplets repeatedly make beat three sound like a new bar. After eight measures, Emi replaces one MOBILE pipe while all four anchors remain untouched. Aiko answers with one dry toy-piano suspended second. Ren replaces the next programmed snare with a single shime-daiko rim stroke. Freya waits until the sub drops out for two bars, then enters with one contrabass-oboe fundamental. At the midpoint, Malik inserts a half-bar vacancy while Emi's chord continues without interruption, making the silence feel like a false edit around an unbroken acoustic breath. The final section returns to the opening drum cell, but the shō lattice is now one pipe different, so the supposedly repeated groove lands in a new harmonic frame.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Frosted greenhouse valve wheel marked 31 beside a translucent grid of six glowing pipe circles, four fixed and two changing, while a footwork beat map beneath it shows several competing downbeat arrows. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “thirty-one,” “same air,” “wrong downbeat,” and “one pipe moved.”

Track 2: Condensation Map Holds Four Anchors
- Story & Context: At 164 BPM, Malik uses the EP's densest kick programming but removes hats during every major shō substitution. Emi sustains four ANCHOR tones through nearly two minutes while cycling three different MOBILE pairs around them. Each new pair creates a different color without fully replacing the chord. Aiko plays only one toy-piano note after each change. Ren uses dry center strokes in place of selected machine claps. Freya contributes short descending semitone figures when the sub is absent. The central break removes all kick and sub for four bars while the shō continues, revealing that the true continuity of the track belongs to the reeds rather than the beat. When the footwork grid returns, its perceived downbeat is shifted by one eighth-note cell.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Condensation streaks on a greenhouse window forming four vertical fixed lines while three pairs of small green marks shift around them, with a dry toy-piano key and shime-daiko rim blurred behind the glass. Spoken fragments limited to “four anchors,” “glass remembers,” “shift the pair,” and “beat comes back sideways.”

Track 3: Intake Lantern Stays Lit Through the Empty Half-Bar
- Story & Context: The closer runs at 152 BPM and begins with the sparsest footwork pattern on the EP. Emi holds a low, dark shō lattice while Malik uses only kick, one clipped snare, and short root sub. Every eight bars he removes exactly half a bar of drums, but Emi never breaks the sustained chord. Aiko remains silent until the second vacancy, then places one muted octave immediately after it. Ren contributes one shime-daiko hit every sixteen bars. Freya holds a long contrabass-oboe note through the third vacancy, creating a two-layer acoustic continuity while the electronic grid disappears. During the last minute, Emi progressively reduces the lattice from six tones to five, then four, then three by removing MOBILE reeds without replacing them. The final half-bar vacancy arrives after the drums have already simplified to almost nothing; only three shō tones remain, sustained through the silence and allowed to stop naturally after the beat has ended.
